// Package dsp is offline, zero-phase signal processing for Go: IIR filter
// design, filtering as (b, a) or second-order sections, frequency response,
// and signal conditioning, matching SciPy and MATLAB.
//
// # Compatibility
//
// Every function matches its SciPy counterpart within a measured tolerance,
// checked against reference vectors generated by a pinned SciPy release. Each
// figure is the worst on either amd64 or arm64, which round slightly
// differently because arm64 fuses multiply-adds.
//
//	Function       SciPy equivalent                    Worst measured   Tested to
//	Butter         butter (b, a)                       7.6e-15          1e-13
//	Butter + SOS   butter(output='sos')                7.3e-15          1e-13
//	ZPK.SOS        zpk2sos                             1.1e-16          1e-14
//	Filter         lfilter                             6.4e-16          1e-14
//	FilterState    lfilter with zi                     2.3e-16          1e-14
//	FilterZi       lfilter_zi                          3.3e-16          1e-14
//	FiltFilt       filtfilt                            8.8e-16          1e-14
//	SOSFilter      sosfilt, with and without zi        4.1e-14          1e-12
//	SOSFilterZi    sosfilt_zi                          9.8e-17          1e-14
//	SOSFiltFilt    sosfiltfilt                         1.7e-13          1e-11
//	Freqz          freqz                               1.4e-15          1e-13
//	SOSFreqz       freqz_sos                           3.7e-13          1e-11
//	Detrend        detrend                             5.6e-16          1e-14
//	Interp1        interp1d(bounds_error=False)        1.3e-16          1e-14
//	RMS            sqrt(mean(x**2)), from NumPy        1.4e-16          1e-14
//
// Errors are relative to the largest expected value, or for filtering to the
// largest input value if that's larger. For scale, 1e-16 is about one unit in
// the last place of a float64. The larger SOS figures all come from one
// deliberately difficult filter (order 8, cutoff 0.01), whose poles sit so close
// to z = 1 that single rounding differences between Go and SciPy are amplified.
// Every other filter agrees within a few units in the last place.
//
// Two very low cutoff filters, order 2 at 0.001 low-pass and 0.0008 high-pass,
// are checked separately. They're the original MATLAB application's own
// designs, and their poles sit even closer to z = 1, so rounding differences
// between implementations are magnified by roughly the square of 1/(1 - pole):
//
//	Function      Worst against SciPy   Tested to   Worst against MATLAB   Tested to
//	Filter        9.5e-13               1e-11       6.2e-13                1e-11
//	FilterState   9.9e-14               1e-12       1.1e-13                1e-11
//	FiltFilt      4.4e-12               1e-10       1.5e-11                1e-9
//	Freqz         1.5e-12               1e-10       1.1e-11                1e-9
//
// SciPy and MATLAB differ from each other on these filters by as much. At DC
// the 0.001 low-pass's gain is exactly 1, and Freqz gives 1 + 1.6e-12 where
// MATLAB gives 1 + 1.3e-11, so much of the difference is MATLAB's.
//
// Functions MATLAB also has are checked against reference vectors generated by
// MATLAB R2025a (Signal Processing Toolbox 25.1), on the same inputs:
//
//	Function                 MATLAB equivalent               Worst measured   Tested to
//	Butter                   [b, a] = butter                 1.2e-13          1e-11
//	Filter                   filter                          6.4e-16          1e-14
//	FilterState              [y, zf] = filter(b, a, x, zi)   3.0e-16          1e-14
//	FiltFilt + MATLABPadLen  filtfilt(b, a, x)               8.8e-16          1e-14
//	SOSFilter                sosfilt                         4.5e-14          1e-12
//	Freqz                    freqz(b, a, n)                  1.9e-15          1e-13
//	SOSFreqz                 freqz(sos, n)                   1.1e-12          1e-10
//	Detrend                  detrend                         1.2e-15          1e-13
//	Interp1                  interp1                         2.2e-16          1e-14
//	FillMissing              fillmissing                     exact            1e-14
//	RMS                      rms                             1.2e-16          1e-14
//
// The largest figures come from the same difficult filter, where MATLAB and
// SciPy differ from each other by as much. Second-order sections aren't
// compared: MATLAB's zp2sos gives a first-order section one zero rather than
// two, and breaks ties in pole radius differently, so it finds the same filter
// in different sections. SOSFiltFilt doesn't yet match MATLAB's
// filtfilt(sos, g, x), which filters section by section. SciPy has no
// fillmissing, so FillMissing is checked against MATLAB's documented definition
// as well as MATLAB itself.
//
// Where MATLAB's behaviour differs from SciPy's, SciPy's is the default and
// MATLAB's is an option, e.g. MATLABPadLen for filtfilt's padding length.
//
// # Filter representations
//
// Design functions return a ZPK: the filter's zeros, poles and gain. Its BA
// method gives transfer-function coefficients (b, a), for Filter and FiltFilt.
// Its SOS method gives second-order sections, for SOSFilter and SOSFiltFilt.
// (b, a) is simple and matches MATLAB's most common usage, but loses precision
// as the order rises or the band narrows. Second-order sections stay accurate,
// and are the better choice above order 4 or so.
//
// # Frequencies
//
// Band edges and cutoffs are normalised to Nyquist, as in SciPy and MATLAB:
// 0 < wn < 1, where 1 is half the sample rate. For a frequency f in Hz at sample
// rate fs, use f / (fs/2). Freqz and SOSFreqz return frequencies in rad/sample,
// also as SciPy and MATLAB do: divide by pi for Nyquist-normalised.
//
// # Conditioning
//
// Detrend removes a signal's least-squares line, or its mean. Interp1
// interpolates linearly, returning NaN outside the sampled range, as MATLAB's
// interp1 and SciPy's interp1d with bounds_error=False do. (numpy.interp
// differs there: it repeats the end values.) FillMissing replaces NaN samples,
// by linear interpolation across each gap and linear extrapolation at the ends,
// or with a constant, as MATLAB's fillmissing. RMS is the root mean square.
//
// # Conventions
//
// Every function is offline and stateless: the whole signal goes in as a
// slice, and a new slice comes back. Inputs are never modified, and nothing
// is shared between calls, so all functions are safe for concurrent use.
// To filter in blocks, FilterState and SOSFilterState take a starting state
// and return the final one, to pass to the next block.
//
// Invalid input returns an error wrapping one of the Err sentinels, so check
// for specific failures with errors.Is. Filtering functions don't check that a
// filter is stable. Like SciPy and MATLAB, they assume it is.
package dsp
